04e468cd" :step/content 17592186167331 :step/next "step-2d471feb-727e-5216-935f-6bc8969b2a48" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2d471feb-727e-5216-935f-6bc8969b2a48" :step/id #uuid "2d471feb-727e-5216-935f-6bc8969b2a48" :step/content 17592186385838 :step/next "step-1f2d63e2-db91-599f-ba79-b154350da4c1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1f2d63e2-db91-599f-ba79-b154350da4c1" :step/id #uuid "1f2d63e2-db91-599f-ba79-b154350da4c1" :step/content 17592186360556 :step/next "step-6bc8b7a0-5c37-5a1c-8102-88692aa5f318" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6bc8b7a0-5c37-5a1c-8102-88692aa5f318" :step/id #uuid "6bc8b7a0-5c37-5a1c-8102-88692aa5f318" :step/content 17592186167332 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078423 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-226f3771-4340-5c2a-b0b8-27bc76c058c5" "question-a939b213-95ee-5354-bca1-280bbeed2f43" "question-947d1f31-89e5-5a9c-9e34-33e153098c01"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078183 :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive ($a_{12}$ goes right after $a_{11}$ in the sequence), we can find the common difference by subtracting $a_{11}$ from $a_{12}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{12}-{a}_{11} \\\\  & =34-39 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078424 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f9d7919f-152a-59f4-84d8-010f7c33087a" "question-ee47bebb-a1a4-5edd-a0dd-08fb92af0f27"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078185 :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if the $12$th term is $-56$ and the $20$th term is $-112.$" :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\na_{\\color{black}12} = {\\color{red}-56}, \\quad a_{\\color{black}20} = {\\color{blue}-112}.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=20$ and $m=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{20}-{a}_{12}}{20-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-112-(-56)}{20-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-112+56}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-56}{8} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-7.$ \n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-226f3771-4340-5c2a-b0b8-27bc76c058c5" :question/id #uuid "226f3771-4340-5c2a-b0b8-27bc76c058c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-5271" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{3}=18$ and ${a}_{4}=23.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{3}$ from ${a}_{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{4}-{a}_{3} \\\\  & =23-18 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8fc7bd26-ca8e-5bc2-bb52-3ac4e12e2471"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8fc7bd26-ca8e-5bc2-bb52-3ac4e12e2471" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fc7bd26-ca8e-5bc2-bb52-3ac4e12e2471" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8618bb86-b61e-563d-b59e-7eac3926ecaf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca7b841c-e04e-5c49-a893-f93f15bfc3ec" "answer-503d308c-1466-5ba5-8ea1-a9767a5440f1" "answer-4fe8b005-e363-529b-8fe2-6986a0457b61" "answer-8618bb86-b61e-563d-b59e-7eac3926ecaf" "answer-7665a2ff-2ced-5664-87cb-3db145a74222"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca7b841c-e04e-5c49-a893-f93f15bfc3ec" :answer/id #uuid "ca7b841c-e04e-5c49-a893-f93f15bfc3ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-503d308c-1466-5ba5-8ea1-a9767a5440f1" :answer/id #uuid "503d308c-1466-5ba5-8ea1-a9767a5440f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fe8b005-e363-529b-8fe2-6986a0457b61" :answer/id #uuid "4fe8b005-e363-529b-8fe2-6986a0457b61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8618bb86-b61e-563d-b59e-7eac3926ecaf" :answer/id #uuid "8618bb86-b61e-563d-b59e-7eac3926ecaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7665a2ff-2ced-5664-87cb-3db145a74222" :answer/id #uuid "7665a2ff-2ced-5664-87cb-3db145a74222" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a939b213-95ee-5354-bca1-280bbeed2f43" :question/id #uuid "a939b213-95ee-5354-bca1-280bbeed2f43" :question/math-academy-id "q-214179" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{7}=6$ and ${a}_{8}=-3.$ The common difference of this sequence is $d=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{7}$ from ${a}_{8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-3-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de38856f-a33a-5ed7-bb38-849a76e1e582"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de38856f-a33a-5ed7-bb38-849a76e1e582" :answer-field/id #uuid "de38856f-a33a-5ed7-bb38-849a76e1e582" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b50083da-beb9-5c72-9c70-0ef2423e7fdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b50083da-beb9-5c72-9c70-0ef2423e7fdd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b50083da-beb9-5c72-9c70-0ef2423e7fdd" :answer/id #uuid "b50083da-beb9-5c72-9c70-0ef2423e7fdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-947d1f31-89e5-5a9c-9e34-33e153098c01" :question/id #uuid "947d1f31-89e5-5a9c-9e34-33e153098c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-103251" :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its sixth and seventh terms are $6$ and $11$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{6}$ from ${a}_{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{7}-{a}_{6} \\\\  & =11-6 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-615f12b4-a2ba-5d4a-9169-34d42046d8b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-615f12b4-a2ba-5d4a-9169-34d42046d8b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "615f12b4-a2ba-5d4a-9169-34d42046d8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12893f67-0a72-530f-9e9f-da2d3170d036" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c65c842-a24a-534b-b71d-7bbd9a2e778b" "answer-6bab63ff-4098-5a0e-800d-2d691a5bd939" "answer-b1f240c5-c25e-5fa7-902e-f55ad2af342c" "answer-8e197a92-1ab8-5d1c-8ddb-f517bf898065" "answer-12893f67-0a72-530f-9e9f-da2d3170d036"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c65c842-a24a-534b-b71d-7bbd9a2e778b" :answer/id #uuid "1c65c842-a24a-534b-b71d-7bbd9a2e778b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bab63ff-4098-5a0e-800d-2d691a5bd939" :answer/id #uuid "6bab63ff-4098-5a0e-800d-2d691a5bd939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1f240c5-c25e-5fa7-902e-f55ad2af342c" :answer/id #uuid "b1f240c5-c25e-5fa7-902e-f55ad2af342c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e197a92-1ab8-5d1c-8ddb-f517bf898065" :answer/id #uuid "8e197a92-1ab8-5d1c-8ddb-f517bf898065" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12893f67-0a72-530f-9e9f-da2d3170d036" :answer/id #uuid "12893f67-0a72-530f-9e9f-da2d3170d036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9d7919f-152a-59f4-84d8-010f7c33087a" :question/id #uuid "f9d7919f-152a-59f4-84d8-010f7c33087a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5274" :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its third and sixth terms are $2$ and $-22$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=2,\\,{a}_{6}=-22.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=6$ and $m=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{6}-{a}_{3}}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-22)-2}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{3} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e18fa7d-c611-527d-89ed-f33f1b215455"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e18fa7d-c611-527d-89ed-f33f1b215455" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e18fa7d-c611-527d-89ed-f33f1b215455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8a16a038-6bbf-5095-975b-dbccda1b5ad7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbf92e30-8f1a-5c5d-abf3-b538bc67a447" "answer-b17c2395-c5ac-59bf-80dd-3db42e983e1a" "answer-8a16a038-6bbf-5095-975b-dbccda1b5ad7" "answer-913157f6-c13c-5150-8233-c83d7cf2170e" "answer-34e48f07-bceb-5ad2-80b0-5117a66ddea5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbf92e30-8f1a-5c5d-abf3-b538bc67a447" :answer/id #uuid "cbf92e30-8f1a-5c5d-abf3-b538bc67a447" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b17c2395-c5ac-59bf-80dd-3db42e983e1a" :answer/id #uuid "b17c2395-c5ac-59bf-80dd-3db42e983e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a16a038-6bbf-5095-975b-dbccda1b5ad7" :answer/id #uuid "8a16a038-6bbf-5095-975b-dbccda1b5ad7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-913157f6-c13c-5150-8233-c83d7cf2170e" :answer/id #uuid "913157f6-c13c-5150-8233-c83d7cf2170e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34e48f07-bceb-5ad2-80b0-5117a66ddea5" :answer/id #uuid "34e48f07-bceb-5ad2-80b0-5117a66ddea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ee47bebb-a1a4-5edd-a0dd-08fb92af0f27" :question/id #uuid "ee47bebb-a1a4-5edd-a0dd-08fb92af0f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-67317" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{20}=21.5$ and ${a}_{24}=12.5.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{20}=21.5,\\,{a}_{24}=12.5.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=24$ and $m=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{24}-{a}_{20}}{24-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5-21.5}{24-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{4} \\\\  & =-2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-2.25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6014564-1e53-5cb5-8d8c-c8d21f1fe08e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6014564-1e53-5cb5-8d8c-c8d21f1fe08e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6014564-1e53-5cb5-8d8c-c8d21f1fe08e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aaf6f79d-d6ee-5f05-a6e5-f7bf8776711a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aaf6f79d-d6ee-5f05-a6e5-f7bf8776711a" "answer-e8157d94-5b61-5154-8e4e-d4a4ee2c3a4b" "answer-488a956f-9bc7-5837-8db3-c481d1b56fc8" "answer-6bced045-23f8-5104-9d2d-7d8e3acb682e" "answer-84168e80-8a3b-5417-900e-1ead231a2125"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaf6f79d-d6ee-5f05-a6e5-f7bf8776711a" :answer/id #uuid "aaf6f79d-d6ee-5f05-a6e5-f7bf8776711a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8157d94-5b61-5154-8e4e-d4a4ee2c3a4b" :answer/id #uuid "e8157d94-5b61-5154-8e4e-d4a4ee2c3a4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-488a956f-9bc7-5837-8db3-c481d1b56fc8" :answer/id #uuid "488a956f-9bc7-5837-8db3-c481d1b56fc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bced045-23f8-5104-9d2d-7d8e3acb682e" :answer/id #uuid "6bced045-23f8-5104-9d2d-7d8e3acb682e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84168e80-8a3b-5417-900e-1ead231a2125" :answer/id #uuid "84168e80-8a3b-5417-900e-1ead231a2125" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079728 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is $6$ and the fifth term is $12.$ Can we use this information to compute the common difference of the sequence?\n\nThe first thing to note is that the sequence is *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/6b/6b82ba58c986aa32635e2ebbd8b1e0fd0affdbc81e35a015960777fa72d5f72b.png)\n\nBecause $a_4 = 6$ and $a_5 = 12$ are **consecutive terms**, meaning that $a_5$ comes straight after $a_4$ in the sequence, we can easily compute the common difference $d$ by subtracting $a_4$ from $a_5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =12-6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the second and fifth terms only of an arithmetic sequence. For example, suppose that we know that\n\n$$\na_2 = {\\color{red}8}, \\quad a_5 = {\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nThese terms are not consecutive, and so how can we figure out the common difference $d?$\n\nFirst, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/ff/ff83c75935acca7ddd5c421cd81fad85685c68fd782d1d832c068237c751753d.png)\n\nWe see that between ${\\color{red}8}$ and ${\\color{red}23}$, there are a total of ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps. So, to find the value of a single jump, we can take the total difference and divide it by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{{\\color{red}23}\\,-\\,{\\color{red}8}}{\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{15}{3} = 5\n$$\n\nWe can check that our result $d=5$ is correct by using it to figure out the intermediate terms. Indeed, the common difference $d=5$ gets us from $a_2={\\color{red}8}$ to $a_5={\\color{red}23}$ in ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps:\n\n![](images/7a/7aa192675aeb3c7be78617035b74362b38974e8501d95c0692238811ae4769f1.png)\n\nIn general, if we're given two terms $a_n$ and $a_m$ of an arithmetic sequence, then we can calculate the common difference using the following formula:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_n-a_m}{n-m}\n$$\n\nUsing this formula to calculate the common difference between $a_{2}=8$ and $a_{5}=23,$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{2}}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-8}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078685 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186171297 :answer-field/choices 17592186171298] [:db/retract 17592186171297 :answer-field/choices 17592186171299] [:db/retract 17592186171297 :answer-field/choices 17592186171300] [:db/retract 17592186171297 :answer-field/choices 17592186171301] [:db/retract 17592186171297 :answer-field/choices 17592186171302] {:db/id 17592186172612 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171297 :answer-field/correct "answer-7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1eb6f98c-b45e-537c-a457-1f57056e9e85" "answer-7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" "answer-90548aa5-959a-5095-a1ec-969585e26d47" "answer-e5262114-a0da-5731-a575-c6db872f7c47" "answer-de84cd40-30b1-5bdf-bf92-d43dfaf0b310"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1eb6f98c-b45e-537c-a457-1f57056e9e85" :answer/id #uuid "1eb6f98c-b45e-537c-a457-1f57056e9e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" :answer/id #uuid "7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90548aa5-959a-5095-a1ec-969585e26d47" :answer/id #uuid "90548aa5-959a-5095-a1ec-969585e26d47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5262114-a0da-5731-a575-c6db872f7c47" :answer/id #uuid "e5262114-a0da-5731-a575-c6db872f7c47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de84cd40-30b1-5bdf-bf92-d43dfaf0b310" :answer/id #uuid "de84cd40-30b1-5bdf-bf92-d43dfaf0b310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c" :question/id #uuid "f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94003" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe3cf3ad-3ee5-5331-8b54-4ce6e5fd60bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe3cf3ad-3ee5-5331-8b54-4ce6e5fd60bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3cf3ad-3ee5-5331-8b54-4ce6e5fd60bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e90c6a79-88fe-5546-8423-a7c541bc4746" "answer-6b14d34e-024d-5c98-a7cd-041c6930d3b2" "answer-5c76486d-0cd9-5267-b109-d5ac0d8c7703" "answer-03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" "answer-a0c17d5d-2d75-57cc-95a7-828178d428fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e90c6a79-88fe-5546-8423-a7c541bc4746" :answer/id #uuid "e90c6a79-88fe-5546-8423-a7c541bc4746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b14d34e-024d-5c98-a7cd-041c6930d3b2" :answer/id #uuid "6b14d34e-024d-5c98-a7cd-041c6930d3b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c76486d-0cd9-5267-b109-d5ac0d8c7703" :answer/id #uuid "5c76486d-0cd9-5267-b109-d5ac0d8c7703" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" :answer/id #uuid "03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0c17d5d-2d75-57cc-95a7-828178d428fa" :answer/id #uuid "a0c17d5d-2d75-57cc-95a7-828178d428fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-64fc3270-931c-55b0-ab11-941f63cb1739" :activity/id #uuid "64fc3270-931c-55b0-ab11-941f63cb1739" :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048493 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-9f77e54a-ce06-5ad1-86c7-0ccf9bdad4ea" "step-c59605b5-04a9-57e2-b8a6-b29858b31bee" "step-2f94d183-1b9a-5126-80a9-56929d485a13" "step-1cb45d40-3dff-5b26-9cf1-cf5ae94915d3" "step-6dd7af92-0a90-5fa3-955d-021bfada8c3b"] :activity/first-step "step-9f77e54a-ce06-5ad1-86c7-0ccf9bdad4ea" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9f77e54a-ce06-5ad1-86c7-0ccf9bdad4ea" :step/id #uuid "9f77e54a-ce06-5ad1-86c7-0ccf9bdad4ea" :step/content 17592186172612 :step/next "step-c59605b5-04a9-57e2-b8a6-b29858b31bee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c59605b5-04a9-57e2-b8a6-b29858b31bee" :step/id #uuid "c59605b5-04a9-57e2-b8a6-b29858b31bee" :step/content "question-f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c" :step/next "step-2f94d183-1b9a-5126-80a9-56929d485a13" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2f94d183-1b9a-5126-80a9-56929d485a13" :step/id #uuid "2f94d183-1b9a-5126-80a9-56929d485a13" :step/content 17592186394880 :step/next "step-1cb45d40-3dff-5b26-9cf1-cf5ae94915d3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1cb45d40-3dff-5b26-9cf1-cf5ae94915d3" :step/id #uuid "1cb45d40-3dff-5b26-9cf1-cf5ae94915d3" :step/content 17592186404619 :step/next "step-6dd7af92-0a90-5fa3-955d-021bfada8c3b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6dd7af92-0a90-5fa3-955d-021bfada8c3b" :step/id #uuid "6dd7af92-0a90-5fa3-955d-021bfada8c3b" :step/content 17592186357637 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a322e8e4-fc5c-530f-aece-0d3557ac2a42" "question-ebf67b7c-064d-5c23-8f05-31612119932c" "question-b09a5298-e405-5905-9c2a-958d82f56eb7" "question-13ad1074-2be7-58b6-8b52-de567a736fd0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188024 :question/problem "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]$ given that $f(2) = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1bcd6d6800caf6abdca868bba4abff3935f26e984807f13c25d4368dc858f2c1.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-6+8) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{4,-4,2\\} = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e747dd9d-512a-59fa-9944-1137ebe8b2f9" "question-1395e862-a7e8-537a-97de-69c4caaca685" "question-a3fba9ae-fcbd-5c75-9ad3-403a4a333328" "question-82901cf9-17f2-500f-9676-c95d18dab327"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188025 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9, 3$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6]$ given that $f(6) = 2.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab98f2f27ff11ddb40138bf09900a865ca006bc8f26c95dfe03ea17fd4e49387.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-6,6),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-5) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(0) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(3-5) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-6}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-9+3-5) \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{2, 7, 4, 13\\} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = 2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = 6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188026 :question/problem "![](images/69/69d2d66022521424f9c6cda6ad901eb9ed1dbdb01044f5fa2d2954049770a3d4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,4].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9,$ $7$ and $8,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]$ given that $f(1) = 10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,4),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-7) \\\\  & =10+7 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-9-7) \\\\  & =10-(-16) \\\\  & =10+16 \\\\  & =26 \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10+8 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{10,17,26,18\\} = 26.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{max} = 26,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is  $x_\\text{max} = -4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188027 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $14$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ given that $f(3) = -2.$\n\n![](images/de/deb5d80fe70d31fe768a180f3afd8a6054eda8e9fc3d1d0cd52c696c9de47660.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-5,5),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-3,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-2) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-14 \\\\  & =-16 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-2+14) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{-2, -4, -16, -14\\} = -16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = -16,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = -3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a322e8e4-fc5c-530f-aece-0d3557ac2a42" :question/id #uuid "a322e8e4-fc5c-530f-aece-0d3557ac2a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-344108" :question/problem "![](images/84/84deebb7aa20aee2bb0b9e57db825fbdfac1482ca4aec7202ba5458c5603492f.png)\n\nThe function $t(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${t}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $t(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$\n\nGiven that $t(2)=3,$ what is the maximum value ${t}_{\\text{max}}$ of $t(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${t}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $t(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${t}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${t}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $t$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2) & =3 \\\\ t(-4) & =t(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =t(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3-(-8) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $t$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $t$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{t}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $t$ is ${t}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $t(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-139d21af-01a8-549d-89ac-a4e1d1e8fbed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-139d21af-01a8-549d-89ac-a4e1d1e8fbed" :answer-field/id #uuid "139d21af-01a8-549d-89ac-a4e1d1e8fbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a" :answer/id #uuid "3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ebf67b7c-064d-5c23-8f05-31612119932c" :question/id #uuid "ebf67b7c-064d-5c23-8f05-31612119932c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344100" :question/problem "![](images/95/955cda48e8e98f46903a471ceefa657a6032cb2e6feab304569a62750eba9825.png)\n\nThe function $r(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,7].$ The graph of ${r}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $r(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$\n\nGiven that $r(7)=6,$ what is the maximum value ${r}_{\\text{max}}$ of $r(x)$ on the interval $[-1,7]?$\n\n${r}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7bf91a3-f797-5574-a5b8-6124d6526096"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7bf91a3-f797-5574-a5b8-6124d6526096" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bf91a3-f797-5574-a5b8-6124d6526096" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1" :answer/id #uuid "87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b09a5298-e405-5905-9c2a-958d82f56eb7" :question/id #uuid "b09a5298-e405-5905-9c2a-958d82f56eb